WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.511
Maths gives you more than one
way to work something out.

2
00:00:07.731 --> 00:00:11.075
Estimate first, then choose the
method that fits the numbers.

3
00:00:11.575 --> 00:00:16.451
Here is a sum to work
out. What is 387 add 214?

4
00:00:16.671 --> 00:00:20.479
Before you calculate, round
each number to something easy.

5
00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:25.576
387 is nearly 400, and
214 is a little past 200.

6
00:00:25.796 --> 00:00:30.579
400 add 200 is 600, so the
answer should be about 600.

7
00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:34.607
An estimate is not the answer.
It shows when one looks silly.

8
00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:39.890
Now work one out exactly. Line the
digits up in their own columns.

9
00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:45.730
Take 347 add 185. Start at
the ones: 7 and 5 make 12.

10
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.758
Write the 2 down, and carry
the ten into the tens column.

11
00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:54.250
The tens are 4, 8 and the carried
ten, which together make 13.

12
00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:59.718
Write 3 and carry a hundred. The
hundreds make 5, so the answer is 532.

13
00:01:00.218 --> 00:01:06.534
Taking away needs the opposite of
carrying. Try 623 take away 187.

14
00:01:06.754 --> 00:01:12.651
3 is less than 7, so exchange
a ten. Now 13 take away 7 is 6.

15
00:01:12.871 --> 00:01:17.980
The tens now hold 1, so exchange
a hundred. 11 take away 8 is 3.

16
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.961
Then 5 take away 1 is 4,
and the answer is 436.

17
00:01:22.461 --> 00:01:26.595
Look at 225 add 98. Some sums
are quicker without columns.

18
00:01:26.815 --> 00:01:32.573
98 is nearly 100. Add 100
instead, and you get 325.

19
00:01:32.793 --> 00:01:38.505
You added 2 too many, so take those
2 away again. That leaves 323.

20
00:01:38.725 --> 00:01:43.834
Work out 4 times 7 times 5.
Multiplying can be regrouped too.

21
00:01:44.054 --> 00:01:49.441
4 times 5 is 20, and 20 times
7 is 140. Much easier that way.

22
00:01:49.941 --> 00:01:54.956
Try 342 times 6. To multiply a
big number, break it into parts.

23
00:01:55.176 --> 00:02:01.724
342 splits into 300, 40 and 2.
Multiply each part on its own.

24
00:02:01.944 --> 00:02:08.121
300 times 6 is 1,800. 40 times
6 is 240. 2 times 6 is 12.

25
00:02:08.341 --> 00:02:14.703
Add the three parts back, and you get
2,052. Miss one and you are short.

26
00:02:14.923 --> 00:02:19.846
What is 87 divided by 5? Dividing
keeps taking whole groups away.

27
00:02:20.066 --> 00:02:26.289
17 fives make 85, and 2 are left
over. So it is 17 remainder 2.

28
00:02:26.789 --> 00:02:30.875
Lay 24 counters out as a
rectangle: 4 rows of 6.

29
00:02:31.095 --> 00:02:35.646
4 and 6 multiply to make 24,
so they are a factor pair.

30
00:02:35.866 --> 00:02:41.068
24 has more pairs: 1 and
24, 2 and 12, and 3 and 8.

31
00:02:41.288 --> 00:02:45.514
Do not stop at the first pair.
Keep going until they repeat.

32
00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:51.864
One fact, two names. 4 is a factor
of 24, and 24 is a multiple of 4.

33
00:02:52.364 --> 00:02:55.847
Estimate first, then work
it out exactly in columns.

34
00:02:56.067 --> 00:03:00.432
Or take a shortcut: give 2 back,
or regroup a multiplication.

35
00:03:00.652 --> 00:03:04.739
Split a big number, share into
fives, and look for factor pairs.
